WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:10.059
1945.

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:12.910
(The battleship Missouri, 53 thousand ton
flagship of Admiral Hasley’s third fleet

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:25.560
becomes the scene of an unforgettable ceremony,
marking the complete and formal surrender

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.560
of Japan)

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.249
Folks, on August 15th 1945, having suffered
the only nuclear bomb attack in history only

00:00:30.249 --> 00:00:34.300
a few days before, the Japanese government
finally surrendered.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:42.989
(As Supreme Commander for the Allied Powers,
I announce it my firm purpose, in the tradition

00:00:42.989 --> 00:00:49.949
of the countries I represent, to proceed in
the discharge of my responsibilities with

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:57.760
justice and tolerance, while taking all necessary
dispositions to insure that the terms of surrender

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.949
are fully, promptly and faithfully complied
with)

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:12.920
At that moment, Japan was a defeated country,
its economy was shattered and the Japanese

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.680
had suffered unimaginable damage.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:21.189
Not only were they immersed in extreme poverty,
they’d also lost more than 3 million compatriots:

00:01:21.189 --> 00:01:23.130
brothers, sons, mothers…

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.350
Wars take a terrible toll.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:30.700
With this grim picture as their situation
in 1945, the question that we might all ask

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:35.620
ourselves is, how on earth did Japan manage
to become one of the greatest economic powers

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.090
on the planet in such a short time?

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:43.650
How did the Japanese recover so quickly and
with such might?

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:53.079
Dear friends, here on VisualPolitik we’ve
already talked about the differences between

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:57.319
the German model and the Japanese model; we’ve
also told you about how Japan transformed

00:01:57.319 --> 00:02:00.789
from a medieval country to a thriving modern
nation.

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:05.329
However, we still need to answer the question
of how Japan managed to become a great world

00:02:05.329 --> 00:02:07.039
power in just a few years.

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:12.700
Let me tell you right now that the Japanese
model is so peculiar, we’ll need more than

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.360
one video to explore it.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.990
In this one we’ll see a few brushstrokes
in the whole picture, but don’t worry, more

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:24.980
chapters will come.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:28.140
(THE POST-WAR)

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:34.930
In 1945, it seemed like everything that had
been built after the Meiji Restoration of

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.269
1868 had evaporated.

00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:41.959
Moreover, as if that weren’t enough, for
the first time in its history, Japan was occupied

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:44.780
by the United States.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:51.599
But wait just a second… because it wasn’t
all bad news.

00:02:51.599 --> 00:02:56.250
As we’ll see, the Japanese also had three
aces up their sleeves, three characteristics

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:01.849
that would launch their country into the stratosphere,
turning it into one of the richest countries

00:03:01.849 --> 00:03:02.849
on the planet.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:06.970
- And that’s in spite of the many political
mistakes that both the occupation forces and

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:10.930
the local authorities would make over the
years.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:19.069
ant to know what these 3 elements were?

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:23.459
The main ingredients of the Japanese recipe?

00:03:23.459 --> 00:03:28.130
Well, first of all, a huge ability and predilection
for saving.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:33.220
See, in Asian countries in general, and Japan
is no exception, families try to save every

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:37.189
penny, much more than in almost any other
place on earth.

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:38.359
Why?

00:03:38.359 --> 00:03:43.670
Well, there are many explanations for this
phenomenon: a cultural preference, a trend

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:47.909
towards less consumption in the present in
order to spend more in the future, or just

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:52.239
because of a lack of public services... which
tend to be very limited in this part of the

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:54.849
world.

00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:58.099
All of these explanations may have a bit of
truth to them...

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:02.920
But regardless, these high amounts of savings
are then invested, which creates productive

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.680
capital that obviously leads to higher production
and wealth levels.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:13.239
It’s savings that allow us to direct our
efforts for a long time, not to satisfy our

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:18.769
immediate needs, but to worry about satisfying
our future needs: we produce capital goods

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:23.900
so that they, in turn, manufacture the future
consumer goods we may need.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:24.900
Juan Ramón Rallo)

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.340
So, you see, the first ace that Japan had
up its sleeve after the war was was an enormous

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.910
propensity to save.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:33.840
Savings, savings, and more savings.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.889
The second had a lot to do with the Meiji
Restoration of 1868.

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:45.139
See, as we saw in a previous video, one of
the first things the Japanese government did

00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:50.960
after the regime change was bet on creating
a very powerful educational system: They established

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:56.800
universal primary education and promoted universities,
academies, research centers, etc., etc.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.870
Thus, after the Second World War, despite
their situation, the Japanese were the most

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:05.210
well trained workers on the entire Asian continent.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:09.710
And of course this entails a huge competitive
advantage.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:16.050
But also, one of the first measures taken
after the war was pushing the educational

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:17.770
system even further.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:18.770
The third card?

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:20.680
Well, I think you all know it.

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:24.240
The Japanese’s huge capacity for and willingness
to work longer hours-- one they’ve always

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.389
had... at least the previous generations.

00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:28.699
And, in some cases, they’ve even taken things
to the extreme.

00:05:28.699 --> 00:05:33.180
Do you know any other country in the world
that has a word for death from working too

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.180
much?

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.180
Listen up.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:39.009
“Karoshi”, death from working too much,
which is a public health problem in Japan.

00:05:39.009 --> 00:05:40.009
BBC)

00:05:40.009 --> 00:05:44.180
The fact is that all three of these factors...
gave Japan a huge capacity to recover, grow,

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.210
and create a lot of wealth.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:51.180
But... just a second… having seen all this,
what stages exactly did Japan go through in

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:55.210
order for this economic miracle to happen?

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:59.980
Listen up.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:04.750
(THE STAGES)

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:08.900
The process we’re about to explore starts
with the US’s occupation of Japan after

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:09.900
the end of the war.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:16.289
See, the occupation forces had two major priorities:
to demilitarize and democratize Japan.

00:06:16.289 --> 00:06:20.930
It’s around this time, for example, that
the new Constitution establishing a democratic

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.169
system was created.

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:26.919
It equalized men’s and women’s rights
and stripped Japan of its right to wage war,

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:28.650
dismantling its Armed Forces.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:32.750
In addition, the occupation forces copied
US institutions and launched reforms that

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:35.760
had some benefits and many drawbacks.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.949
- I think one day we’ll need to talk about
how badly the military has always managed

00:06:40.949 --> 00:06:43.970
the economy of the countries they’ve controlled.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:49.010
The fact is that the priority of these reforms
was to eliminate, or at least reduce, the

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:53.240
power of the oligarchs who’d supported Japanese
expansionism and militarism.

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:57.870
To do so, the sale of land from big landowners
to small farmers was approved.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.850
Measures were taken to dissolve the Zaibatsu,
large business groups that engaged in many

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:04.620
activities but were controlled by a single
family.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.360
An antitrust law that outlawed monopolies
was also passed in 1947, and the unionization

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.509
of workers was promoted.

00:07:10.509 --> 00:07:17.540
Everything, I insist, was done with the intention
of achieving an economic power change.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:21.430
Of course, some of these measures, in addition
to limiting the power of the great oligarchs,

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:23.849
also had some rather positive consequences:

00:07:23.849 --> 00:07:28.360
For example, the land distribution managed
to turn many farmers into owners; the demise

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:33.240
of many of the Zaibatsu led to the emergence
of many small and medium enterprises.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.900
In spite of everything, their recovery was
much slower than expected…

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:44.569
However, in the 1950s the country had, as
it were, a stroke of luck... the Korean War

00:07:44.569 --> 00:07:49.360
turned Japan into one of the alliance forces’
suppliers... thereby boosting their exports

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:50.360
and production.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:57.009
Suddenly, Japanese companies – and politicians
– discovered the great global market.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:08:01.050
In 1953, production levels had already surpassed
the levels they’d been at right before the

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:08.800
war, and the political class had a new main
priority: boosting economic growth through

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:12.169
business.

00:08:12.169 --> 00:08:17.150
(THE ECONOMIC TAKEOFF)

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:24.689
Folks, the decades of the 50s, 60s, and even
70s, were miraculous years, Japan’s golden

00:08:24.689 --> 00:08:25.689
age.

00:08:25.689 --> 00:08:30.650
A time led by the “Liberal Democratic Party”
a political party that ruled the country for

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.990
almost 40 uninterrupted years

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:37.419
- A few years ago, by the way, it was discovered
that the CIA gave a lot of money to support

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:39.110
the party’s political campaigns.

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:48.680
This is also the party of the current Abe
Minister, Shinzo Abe.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:51.970
The fact is that this party launched a very
peculiar system:

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:56.440
See, on the one hand, it promoted policies
that favored saving, kept inflation under

00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:01.370
control, slowed down the growth of the public
sector, maintained low taxes and avoided the

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:02.759
accumulation of debt.

00:09:02.759 --> 00:09:11.100
- This is the opposite of what they do in
countries like Argentina or Italy, for example.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:16.890
However, just a second, because that’s not
all.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:20.980
On the other hand, unlike what happened in
other countries that achieved a great economic

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:24.470
takeoff, the truth is that these governments
were quite interventionist:

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:28.720
For example, they decided to promote certain
industries, which they themselves chose and

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.950
which they helped grow from within the government
itself;

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:35.630
They also promoted a new cartelization of
national companies...

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:39.730
supposedly to... avoid what they considered
to be excessive competition...

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:40.730
- (Gesture:?)

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:45.300
Although I don’t know… it sounds like
it was mainly a way to help out their friends.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:52.320
Regardless, it had amazing results.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.420
In the early 1960s, Prime Minister Hayato
Ikeda announced a plan to double the size

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:58.630
of Japan's economy in just 10 years.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:03.910
To reach this goal the country needed to maintain
an annual average growth of 7.2% for that

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:05.050
entire time.

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:07.250
International analysts considered it impossible.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:09.870
10 years later, Japan had far exceeded its
goal.

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:14.200
The country had multiplied the size of its
economy by 2.8 because it had an average annual

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:18.680
growth of more than 9.3%.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:28.380
Thus, in 1973, the Japanese economy had multiplied
by more than 5 since 1953, that is, in just

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.250
20 years.

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:34.589
On the other hand, GDP per capita multiplied
by 4 and within a few years Japan became the

00:10:34.589 --> 00:10:39.130
largest asset holder in the world and the
largest humanitarian aid provider.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:44.060
Even the Tokyo Stock Exchange managed to outperform
the New York Stock Exchange for a short period

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:45.060
of time.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:46.060
But... you know what, folks?

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:49.240
This is when a great controversy arose…

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:56.850
On one side, some defend the country’s spectacular
growth despite all these government policies.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:11:00.141
That is to say, the 3 factors that we covered
at the beginning of the video-- Savings, work

00:11:00.141 --> 00:11:04.820
and training-- were so strong that they managed
to take off in spite of the government’s

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:05.820
interference.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:09.930
But some argue that since the government kept
the macroeconomic parameters under control,

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:15.550
these public policies actually accelerated
the country's growth.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:20.970
By the way, which group do you belong to?

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:24.540
Leave your answer in the comments section
and also in this survey.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:29.350
What there’s little doubt about, is that
it was exactly during that time that some

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:34.149
of the great imbalances that led the country
into a huge crisis, from which it hasn’t

00:11:34.149 --> 00:11:35.470
yet recovered, emerged.

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:38.960
But we already talked about that in this other
video.

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:43.351
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

